From: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: ppe: offload the reply direction
 towards DSA user ports

Only the SNAT direction of a masqueraded connection was accelerated so
far: the reply, a separate flow_cls_offload with its own cookie, was
declined twice over, once as "not snat-outbound" and once because its
redirect target is a DSA user port (lanN), not an EDMA netdevice. Every
download therefore crossed the CPU.

Accept single-ended NAT in both shapes. The flowtable builds the reply
of a masquerade as "destination rewritten, source unchanged" (the
inbound direction of a port forward has the same shape, its reply the
SNAT shape), which the parameterised NAT encoder already covers with
PPE_FLOW_FWD_DNAT: the nexthop carries the new destination address in
its dnat_ip field and the flow entry the new destination port. The host
table entry is the original source for both shapes -- the LAN client
of an SNAT flow, the remote peer of a DNAT flow, marked WAN -- and the
flow entry's own address the destination. The entry format allows the
other assignment for DNAT (the public address as the host), and that
was tried first: measured on a GL-BE9300 the flows showed as offloaded
while every packet still crossed the CPU, on both taggers. No public IP
table entry is taken for a DNAT flow. Hairpin NAT (both ends rewritten)
and plain routing (neither) stay declined. The fail-closed check that
declined any destination-port rewrite goes with the SNAT-only gate it
belonged to; what replaces it pins the port on the side that must not
change for each shape (the destination port of an SNAT flow, the source
port of a DNAT flow), so an entry is only ever programmed with the one
port rewrite it will actually perform, the one the flowtable asked for.

A redirect to a DSA user port is resolved, mirroring
mtk_flow_get_dsa_port(), to the conduit's PPE port plus the tag inserts
the nexthop table offers (one C-tag, one S-tag) that make the switch
deliver the frame to that port, since hardware cannot run the tagger:

  - tag_8021q (vsc73xx-8021q): a C-tag with the port's tag_8021q VID.
    For a port of a VLAN-unaware bridge that is the bridge VID, which is
    what the software tagger sends straight onto a bridged port too
    (pending-6.18/766): the switch picks the egress port from its own
    FDB, so a client that moves ports is followed, and a miss floods no
    further than the bridge's members. An unbridged port gets its
    standalone VID.
  - rtl8_4: the head tag emulated as S-tag TCI 0x0400 (protocol 0x04,
    reason 0) plus C-tag TCI BIT(port) (ALLOW=0, forwarding port mask),
    which the aliased egress TPIDs (S=0x8899, C=0x8100) turn into
    0x8899 0400 8100 <mask>. For the S-tag to come out at all the
    conduit has to be an S-VLAN egress port: with the port left a
    C-VLAN port (its reset type) the switch delivered nothing for such
    flows, with the type bit set the same flows ran at 2353 Mbit/s, and
    the flood path, DHCP and ARP were unaffected either way -- so the
    alias program in edma_port.c now sets that bit on the conduit while
    the alias holds and clears it when it leaves. The switch accepts
    word 2 as 0x8100 (a C-TPID of 0x00a0, the software tagger's flags,
    changed nothing). Read by the switch as its flag byte pair, that
    word is EFID_EN=1/EFID 0, PRI_EN=0, KEEP=0, LEARN_DIS=0, none of
    which an explicit forwarding port mask consults. The port is always
    named outright: an untagged frame would be classified to the CPU
    port's PVID, the seeded VLAN 1 that every port is a member of under
    this tagger, and an FDB miss would flood a routed frame across
    every bridge on the switch.

Only the DSA structures of the redirect target decide the port; the
switch's FDB is never read. Declined: a port of a VLAN-aware bridge, a
port behind a LAG conduit, a VLAN push on top of a DSA port, a tagger
without an encapsulation here, and a conduit whose parser (and so the
chip-wide egress TPIDs, edma_port.c) is not programmed for that tagger
-- the parser state enum moves to edma_port.h for that check, and
Kconfig gains the BRIDGE dependency the bridge checks need. The
Ethernet mangle's source must be the conduit's MAC, which its user
ports and, by default, their bridge inherit. The hairpin decline, the
FLOW_CLS_STATS path and the TCP control-flag exceptions are unchanged;
with both directions in hardware the exceptions are now the only thing
that returns a FIN or RST to software, in either direction.

A DNAT flow is accepted only when the address it translates is this
router's own on the interface (or VLAN upper) the packets arrive
through (__ip_dev_find()). The rule carries no trace of an
encapsulation the flowtable cannot express, so a PPPoE WAN's reply
would otherwise be programmed as a plain-IPv4 entry keyed on the raw
WAN port, which a spoofed plain frame on the WAN segment could hit
while software drops it.

The reply on a tagged WAN arrives with a FLOW_DISSECTOR_KEY_VLAN match
on the real device and a FLOW_ACTION_VLAN_POP. The flowtable fills the
key in but does not mark it in used_keys (nf_flow_rule_match(),
v6.18), so flow_rule_match_key() alone is blind to it and the pop would
be declined as unmatched -- measured: every wan.10 reply declined on
both taggers; the dissector offset, set together with the key, is the
second signal. Hardware keys a flow on the ingress port's L3 interface,
not the VLAN, so this is accepted with the same collapse the LAN side
already documents (a flow entered through wan.10 also matches the
5-tuple arriving untagged on wan, and vice versa), only when the VID
names a VLAN upper really stacked on that device, and never on a DSA
user port. It is accepted under the tag_8021q tagger and declined while
the rtl8_4 parser alias holds the chip-wide ingress C-TPID at 0x0000:
measured, such flows sat in hardware and never hit (the tag is not
parsed as a VLAN at all), so they are declined and logged rather than
programmed for nothing.

Measured on a GL-BE9300 with a 2.5 Gbit/s peer on the WAN and a
2.5 Gbit/s client on lan3, CPU idle from /proc/stat, software path
about 74% idle. With tag_8021q, on the final build: download
2353 Mbit/s in hardware, download through a VLAN-tagged WAN 2347,
upload 2347; on the preceding build of the same tag_8021q path: upload
2343 Mbit/s at 99.4%, tagged-WAN upload 2343, a DNAT port forward 2353
and 2349 Mbit/s both directions in hardware, 40 teardowns leaving
nothing ESTABLISHED, VLAN regression suite 4/4. With rtl8_4, the default
tagger, from a cold boot with nothing else set: download 2353 Mbit/s
at 99.3% idle, upload 2350 Mbit/s at 99.5%, tagged-WAN upload
2344 Mbit/s, port forward 2353 and 2352 Mbit/s, 30 short connections
leaving conntrack clean and the VLAN suite 4/4 (DHCP renewals were
unaffected on the preceding build); tagged-WAN download stays on the
software path (1454 Mbit/s at 74.4% idle) with the decline logged once.
Frames forged on a LAN port with a 0x8899 head of their own arrive at
the conduit as data inside the switch's genuine head tag; ten WAN
frames of each kind that could exploit the aliased TPIDs, carrying a
live flow's 5-tuple, reached neither the flow's port nor lan1 and left
the flow untouched (the port their mask named was not watched yet).

Note on the tagged-WAN reply match: nf_flow_rule_match() never sets the
VLAN key in used_keys, so the reply direction of a flow through a VLAN
upper is recognised through the dissector's key offset instead (meta is
first in struct nf_flow_key, so a VLAN key at offset 0 is "absent"). That
leans on an internal netfilter layout; if it changes the driver declines
such flows (fail closed, pr_debug trail) rather than mis-matching them.
The used_keys gap is worth fixing in nf_flow_rule_match() upstream.

Signed-off-by: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/ppe_offload.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/ppe_offload.c
@@ -14,15 +14,31 @@
