  *   and hands the tag over as the push, which the nexthop's C-VLAN
  *   tag-insert fields carry (a redirect to the VLAN netdevice itself is
@@ -30,17 +46,25 @@
  *   The push must match a VLAN upper really stacked on that device,
  *   whose MTU then bounds the flow's PMTU; a flow back out of its own
  *   ingress port, a DSA conduit as egress, and a push with a PCP are
- *   declined. A
- *   second/S-tag push, or any other VLAN proto than 802.1Q, is
+ *   declined. A second/S-tag push, or any other VLAN proto than 802.1Q, is
  *   declined -- untested, and this hardware's nexthop entry only has
- *   a single C-VLAN + single S-VLAN insert field to begin with. Bridge
- *   and DSA (rtl837x) netdevices themselves are still out of scope --
- *   on this board's LAN side, real traffic's egress device is
- *   typically a DSA/bridge netdevice, not an EDMA one (or a VLAN on
- *   top of one), so this milestone in practice only accelerates the
- *   WAN-side direction of traffic through a plain (or VLAN-tagged)
- *   EDMA netdevice. Extending this to DSA/bridge devices (mirroring
- *   mtk_flow_get_dsa_port()) is follow-up work.
+ *   a single C-VLAN + single S-VLAN insert field to begin with.
+ * - A DSA user port as egress (the LAN side of this board, mirroring
+ *   mtk_flow_get_dsa_port()) is programmed as the conduit's PPE port,
+ *   and the switch is told which port the frame is for through the
+ *   nexthop's tag inserts, since hardware cannot run the tagger:
+ *   see ppe_offload_dsa_egress(). The port is taken from the DSA
+ *   structures of the redirect target only; a port of a VLAN-aware
+ *   bridge, a port behind a LAG conduit, a VLAN push on top of a DSA
+ *   port, a tagger this code has no encapsulation for, and a conduit
+ *   whose parser is not programmed for that tagger are all declined.
+ * - Tagged ingress (the reply direction on a tagged WAN) arrives as a
+ *   FLOW_DISSECTOR_KEY_VLAN match on the real device plus a
+ *   FLOW_ACTION_VLAN_POP. The flow key cannot carry the VLAN, only the
+ *   port's ingress L3 interface, so this is accepted with the collapse
+ *   described under SECURITY LIMITATION below, and only when the VID
+ *   names a VLAN upper really stacked on that device. Tagged ingress on
+ *   a DSA user port (a VLAN-aware bridge) is declined.
  * - The flow must carry a FLOW_ACTION_MANGLE for the Ethernet header:
  *   this is the *only* source this code uses for the nexthop's
  *   destination MAC, deliberately -- the kernel has already resolved
@@ -64,21 +88,30 @@
  *   accelerated, leaving conntrack holding closed connections at
  *   timeouts[ESTABLISHED] instead of timeouts[CLOSE].
  *
- *   NOTE for whoever implements reply-direction offload: today a
- *   remote peer's FIN/RST always traverses the software path anyway,
- *   because the reply direction is declined, and that is what sets
- *   NF_FLOW_CLOSING. The exceptions are therefore currently a second
- *   line of defence for the LAN-initiated case (an RST abort draws no
- *   reply). Once the reply direction IS offloaded they become the only
- *   mechanism -- do not weaken them in that milestone.
- *
- *   Also for that milestone: while an rtl8_4 conduit aliases the parser
- *   (edma_port.c), the global C-TPID is 0x0000. On the WAN port the
- *   hardware then parses L3 four bytes after an EtherType of 0x0000 --
- *   a frame Linux treats as 802.3 length and never as IP. Today that can
- *   only end in a CPU punt; once WAN-ingress flows are offloaded, either
- *   make the WAN port refuse such frames or verify the parser still
- *   matches 0x8100 natively and revisit the TPID choice.
+ *   With both directions of a connection in hardware these exceptions
+ *   are the ONLY thing that returns a FIN or RST to software, in either
+ *   direction: nothing else sets NF_FLOW_CLOSING. Do not weaken them.
+ *
+ *   While an rtl8_4 conduit aliases the parser (edma_port.c), the
+ *   chip-wide ingress C-TPID is 0x0000. On the WAN port the hardware
+ *   then parses L3 four bytes after an EtherType of 0x0000 -- a frame
+ *   Linux treats as 802.3 length and never as IP -- and such a frame
+ *   can hit an offloaded WAN-ingress flow. That adds no exposure a
+ *   plain IP frame with the same 5-tuple does not already have: the
+ *   flow forwards either, and the nexthop's tag inserts replace
+ *   whatever was parsed (measured for the WAN egress, expected for the
+ *   conduit -- the same expectation covers a WAN frame carrying the
+ *   aliased 0x8899 S-TPID with an attacker-chosen TCI, which must not
+ *   survive into the emulated head tag -- measured so far: ten such
+ *   frames of each kind carrying a live flow's 5-tuple from the WAN,
+ *   the S+C kind naming lan2 in its mask, reached neither the flow's
+ *   client port nor lan1, and the flow they mimicked ran on; lan2 and
+ *   the CPU port were not watched, which a follow-up capture has to
+ *   cover before this counts as settled), so it is left alone. A real
+ *   0x8100 tag on the WAN is NOT parsed while the C-TPID is aliased
+ *   (measured: such flows sat in hardware and never hit), so a tagged
+ *   ingress is declined while the alias is active rather than
+ *   programmed for nothing.
  *
  * - SECURITY LIMITATION: a flow entry is keyed on the 5-tuple plus an
  *   ingress L3 interface, and nothing finer. ppe_offload_ingress_edma_dev()
@@ -90,28 +123,28 @@
  *   host in the weaker zone can source-spoof a victim's offloaded
  *   5-tuple and have silicon forward and translate its packets with no
  *   software inspection -- no rp_filter, no interface-scoped rule. The
+ *   same holds for the VLANs of the WAN port once tagged ingress is
+ *   accepted: a flow entered through wan.10 also matches the 5-tuple
+ *   arriving untagged on wan, and vice versa. The
  *   hardware offers no finer qualifier, so this is a property of
  *   enabling the flowtable across such a boundary, not something this
  *   driver can validate away.
  *
- * Hardware itself may also not consult the flow table at all yet for
- * genuinely external ingress traffic -- ppe_servcode_init() bypasses
- * flow lookup (PPE_SC_BYPASS_INGRESS_FLOW_LOOKUP) only for service
- * code 1, which edma_tx.c applies solely to CPU-originated egress
- * descriptors, not to arriving port traffic, so external ingress
- * should default to an unbypassed (zeroed) bitmap and see flow lookup
- * -- but this has not been confirmed against a live register read.
- * That confirmation, along with everything else in this comment, is a
- * hardware-testing-time question; nothing here changes it.
+ * Flow lookup on genuinely external ingress is a given by now: flows
+ * entering on the WAN port and on the conduit both hit in hardware
+ * (measured, both taggers).
  */
 
